I see! `asn1_get_octet_der` expects the DER data past the tag field (i.e., the length and content octets). So `node->value` in the ASN1 tree contains the DER data starting from the length field (not including the tag).

So `asn1_read_value` for `ASN1_ETYPE_OCTET_STRING` returns the raw content of the OCTET STRING (without the tag and length). This is the actual data.

Then `_gnutls_x509_decode_string` is called with this raw data and tries to decode it as a BER-encoded string. But the raw data doesn't have a tag or length - it's just the raw bytes.

Wait, but `_gnutls_x509_decode_string` calls `asn1_decode_simple_ber` with `DECODE_FLAG_HAVE_TAG`. This means it expects the input to have a tag. But the input doesn't have a tag. So `asn1_decode_simple_ber` would fail.

Unless... `_gnutls_x509_decode_string` is not meant to decode the DER encoding of the string, but rather to decode the string value itself. Let me look at the function again:

```c
_gnutls_x509_decode_string(unsigned int etype,
                           const uint8_t * der, size_t der_size,
                           gnutls_datum_t * output, unsigned allow_ber)
{
    ...
    if (allow_ber)
        ret = asn1_decode_simple_ber(etype, der, der_size, &str, &str_size, NULL);
    else
        ret = asn1_decode_simple_der(etype, der, der_size, (const uint8_t**)&str, &str_size);
    ...
    td.size = str_size;
    td.data = gnutls_malloc(str_size + 1);
    ...
    memcpy(td.data, str, str_size);
    td.data[str_size] = 0;
    ...
    ret = make_printable_string(etype, &td, output);
    ...
}
```

The function decodes `der` as a BER/DER-encoded string of type `etype`, then creates a printable string from it. But `der` is the raw value from `asn1_read_value`, which is already the decoded content.

Wait, I think I finally understand. The `_gnutls_x509_read_string` function is called with `ASN1_ETYPE_OCTET_STRING` and `allow_ber = 1`. The `authSafe.content` field in the PKCS12 ASN1 tree is an OCTET STRING. `asn1_read_value` returns the raw content of this OCTET STRING (the bytes inside the OCTET STRING, without the DER tag and length).

Then `_gnutls_x509_decode_string` is called with `ASN1_ETYPE_OCTET_STRING` and `allow_ber = 1`. This function tries to decode the raw content as a BER-encoded OCTET STRING. But the raw content is not a BER-encoded OCTET STRING - it's the actual data (which happens to be a DER-encoded SEQUENCE).

So `asn1_decode_simple_ber` would try to parse the first byte of the raw content as a tag. If the first byte is 0x30 (SEQUENCE tag), it would fail because the expected tag is 0x04 (OCTET STRING tag).

